summary:
I read every WindowCases.ps1 hunk in full and the rest of the slice through its diffs or whole files. For most of what the design asked, the build delivers it, and what I ran passed:
- the 46 W001Proof, W001Oracle and WindowCasesHelper tests;
- Fitness op=check (all rules hold; the docs/proof-runs rule is awaiting its file, as designed);
- tests/bugs/SL-107-RecordPathRule.Tests.ps1 (all checks held);
- tests/window/SL-003-W001OnASubstDrive.Tests.ps1 end to end on a SUBST letter over a 30,302-folder temp tree. The finished run held. The stopped run stopped at 17,602 folders with sized '-' and held. Nothing was left under .taad, and the SUBST letter, the deny ACEs and the record were all removed afterwards.

I could not re-run the rules harness: no interpreter with the yaml package was found for the engine checkout.

The defects are in the error paths that turn a run's ending into a verdict, where the code departs from the design's contracts. I reproduced each one by calling the script's own functions with stubbed launches:
1. W-003 real mode, Stop pressed but the app reaches no ending within 600 s: the record says 'inconclusive' and the case exits 0 PASSED. The contract says FAILED.
2. W-003 real mode, the hook refuses the marker: the case exits 0 PASSED, not INCONCLUSIVE as O-003 decided.
3. W-001 -Stopped, a stop attempt that times out after the hook acted: it is retried, and the stopped part can end 'held'. The contract says failed.
4. W-001 -Stopped still runs the stopped scan after a finished run that did not end Completed. The design says no stopped part then.
5. W-002 on a scan that is not Completed: the case FAILS while its record and decision n1 say INCONCLUSIVE.

W-002 and W-003 on their rigs remain unrun, as does the maintainer's whole-C:\ run.

Second reader, for inputs: Inputs pass over SL-003's new readers, classifiers and record helpers in tests/window/WindowCases.ps1. Every finding below was run, not reasoned.

1. Major. W-001 -Stopped retries an attempt that ended TIMEOUT, crashed or left no report as if it were only "not partial". Only the last attempt's exit code is checked, so a Stop that hung the app for 3600 s still ends 'held' when the retry at half the folders comes back clean. The design says a stopped run that timed out is FAILED. A stubbed run reproduced it: attempts=2, verdict=held, failures=0.

2. Minor. Get-W001RootShape classifies the root as typed, not as the app normalises it:
   - 'C:/', 'C:\\' and 'C:\.' come out 'folder', and '\\?\C:\' comes out 'unc';
   - the app scans each of them as the whole C:\;
   - so a whole-C:\ run skips the coverage and reason checks and writes its record to artifacts instead of docs/proof-runs.

3. Minor. Seam.ListFiles has the same gap: a root with '/' separators or a trailing '\.' lists 0 files, so W-002 judges the scan against an empty listing.

4. Minor. Test-W003Real waits with [Console]::In.ReadLineAsync(). That call blocks until a line arrives (measured: it returned after 3.8 s), so the seconds-since-launch counter O-003 promised never shows.

5. Noted, not sent back. The refused-another-code class also counts network and device errors, which come before any access check, so a share that drops after the scan fails W-001 on a network root.

All three repros were compiled and run with eng/dotnet.ps1 test and failed for the stated reason. Their files were then removed, and the tree is back to the engine's own files.

Checked and found sound:
- Read-ActedLine on both line forms, including a path in current= that holds ' folders=' or ' ms=';
- the hook's real armed and refusal line texts (StartupHook.cs:595, :611, :739);
- RowsMode's 'rows read' line;
- Read-RowsDump on zero rows (an empty array, not @($null));
- UnreadableRows.Reason, which is one line per code and never depends on the kind, so Get-ReasonCodeMismatches does not fire falsely;
- every ScanStatus and outcome value, all in the writer's allowed set;
- the record's depth against -Depth 6;
- Get-CountsOnly on ConvertFrom-Json objects;
- CfProvider's 'CB FETCH_DATA pid=' format;
- the guard runner's YAML reader against the engine-appended plain scalars.

Reading pass: I read every range the engine listed, and nothing in them needs a new finding.
- `eng/rules-harness/harness.py`, lines 590-607: the `load_awaiting` docstring now names the SL-003 rule and `docs/proof-runs/w001-c-drive.json`. That matches the `awaiting_file` key and value in `plants.json`. The code in the function did not change.
- `tests/bugs/SL-107-RecordPathRuleCorpus.json`, lines 1-10: the `rule` field now carries the new rule name word for word, the same name as in `.taad/fitness.yaml` and in the runner's `$RuleName`. The runner passed when I ran it earlier.
- `.taad/fitness.yaml`, lines 1469-1504: these are the three entries the engine appended, and the builder left them unchanged. In the proof-run record rule, `/` and `\\(?!["u])|\\u00(?:5[cC]|2[fF])` are plain YAML scalars, which YAML reads exactly as written. That makes them the same regexes as SL-107's single-quoted ones. Fitness op=check passed, with this rule waiting for its file as designed.
- `eng/rules-harness/plants.json`, lines 1-8 and 243-259: S91-S94 and P41 re-plant the SL-107 texts unchanged at `docs/proof-runs/w001-c-drive.json`, under the new rule name. S95 and P42 anchor on a line that exists in `WindowCases.ps1` (`$script:Failures = ...`, line 179), and their texts can fire, or stay quiet under, `\.taad[\\/]+slices`.

The only thing that reads oddly is in the `_note`: its "156 rows" count and its mention of P36 among the quiet rows are out of date. The count was already wrong before this slice (162 rows before, 164 now), and the note itself says S91-S94 and P41 replace the old rows. So it is not new here.

